; The four claims of arch.claims, re-asked as derivations (extension §2) —
; and the blueprint extraction the claims file cannot express at all.
;
; This file is CHECKED AGAINST arch.claims by modality-cross-check.sh, not a
; replacement for it: two implementations of one question, so a disagreement is
; a bug in one of them rather than a number to adjust.
;
; POLARITY — read this before "fixing" anything below.
;   possible F   the relation is the SATISFYING set      non-empty ⟺ holds
;   live F       the relation is the COUNTEREXAMPLE set   empty     ⟺ holds
;   never F      the relation is the COUNTEREXAMPLE set   empty     ⟺ holds
; `never` is `possible` negated, so its relation collects the situations that
; SATISFY the forbidden guard. Writing it as the satisfying set of the safe
; guard would invert the oracle while leaving the script green.

(load "ct.rules")

; `possible`: the finished architectures — the satisfying set, and the answer to
; the `realisable` property in corpus.claims. One guard, one name: it was
; declared twice before, as `realisable` and again as `finished`, differing only
; in an `(init S) (reach S T)` premise that `(situation T)` already implies
; (§12.3 enumerates the reachable situations and no others).
;
; The surviving name is `finished` because it is not private to this file:
; demo.sh derives it to pick a design, and pol-c4's usage tells a reader to run
; `pol derive MODEL RULES finished` to list them. A relation other programs call
; by name is an interface, so the deduplication keeps THAT side of the pair.
(satisfies finished (is cur.at done))

; `live`: the counterexample set — reachable situations from which no finished
; architecture is reachable. Empty, because every admissible prefix extends.
(can-reach can-finish finished)
(trapped no-dead-end can-finish)

; `never`: the counterexample sets — situations that satisfy what is forbidden.
(satisfies rerun-is-affordable (and (defined classify.chosen)
                                    (not (is extract.chosen.persists yes))))

(satisfies crm-stays-loose (is surface.chosen direct-db))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; READING THE DESIGN OUT. This is what a query cannot do.
;
; `(holds S (is K.chosen C))` with S and K bound DERIVES C — interrogator §2 is
; explicit that this is the only way to ask "in situation S, where does this
; arrow point", because G is a guard datum whose free variables are the rule's,
; over a mutable cell. The same question as a `.claims` query answers empty.
(relation choice (Situation cap comp))
(rule (choice S K C) (situation S) (holds S (is K.chosen C)))

; stage -> part, for finished architectures only: the machine-readable artifact.
;   pol derive arch.pol arch.rules "(blueprint 183 K C)"
(relation blueprint (Situation cap comp))
(rule (blueprint S K C) (finished S) (choice S K C))

; The half of the answer set that satisfies every STATED requirement and still
; makes re-classification cost a full re-extract.
(relation rerun-expensive (Situation))
(rule (rerun-expensive S)
  (finished S)
  (choice S extract C)
  (is C.persists no))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE DIAGRAM'S DATA CONTRACT — what `pol-c4` reads, and all it reads.
;
; Narrow relations rather than one wide join on purpose: a component missing a
; presentation attribute should lose that ONE field of its box, not vanish from
; the diagram entirely. A vacatable arrow that is vacant produces no row.

(relation box-tech     (Situation cap technology))
(relation box-level    (Situation cap c4))
(relation box-external (Situation cap flag))
(relation box-within   (Situation cap boundary))

(rule (box-tech S K T)     (blueprint S K C) (is C.tech T))
(rule (box-level S K L)    (blueprint S K C) (is C.level L))
(rule (box-external S K E) (blueprint S K C) (is C.external E))
(rule (box-within S K B)   (blueprint S K C) (is C.within B))

; RUNTIME data flow, which is not the build ladder — see arch.lib.pol.
(relation dataflow (cap cap protocol))
(rule (dataflow A B V) (is F.f-from A) (is F.f-to B) (is F.f-via V))

; C4's context level draws people.
(relation actor-of (actor cap))
(rule (actor-of A K) (is A.drives K))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY NOT THE OTHERS — what `pol derive --why` cannot tell you.
;
; The derivation tree for a chosen part proves only that it WAS chosen. A
; decision record has to say what was rejected and on which demand, and that is
; a different question: not "how do I know this fact" but "which candidates did
; the guard exclude". One relation per demand, so the reason is in the name.

(relation candidate (cap comp))
(rule (candidate K C) (is P.p-cap K) (is P.p-comp C))

(relation rejected-bulk (cap comp))
(rule (rejected-bulk K C) (candidate K C) (is K.needs-bulk yes) (not (is C.bulk yes)))

(relation rejected-scan (cap comp))
(rule (rejected-scan K C) (candidate K C) (is K.needs-scan yes) (not (is C.scans yes)))

(relation rejected-rerun (cap comp))
(rule (rejected-rerun K C) (candidate K C) (is K.needs-rerun yes) (not (is C.rerunnable yes)))

(relation rejected-coupling (cap comp))
(rule (rejected-coupling K C) (candidate K C) (is K.needs-loose yes) (is C.couples tight))
